# Curated artefact -- Phase 4, QHV leg, MECHANISM CONTROL (2026-09-09).
# QEMU 6.2.0 (distro), -cpu cortex-a57 (ARMv8.0: EL2 present, NO VHE, no PAuth), blk only,
# no rng, -snapshot; one run, 240 s ceiling.
# RESULT, two parts:
#   HOST: gets PAST the point where "-cpu max" hangs on this same QEMU/image/board. The
#         waitfor timeout message ("Unable to access /dev/random") fires, networking starts,
#         post_start runs, qvm is launched. Same QEMU, same bytes, same board -- the only
#         change is that the CPU model has no VHE, so QNX's EL2 host cannot take the E2H=1
#         path whose timer (the EL2 *virtual* timer) 6.2's virt board never wires to the GIC.
#         This is the non-VHE control the review asked for; it SUPPORTS the mechanism for
#         the host hang. It does not observe register state and is a single run.
#   GUEST: does not come up -- "PE does not support PAUTH feature". The guest's startup
#         (startup-armv8_fm) requires Pointer Authentication (ARMv8.3); Cortex-A57 is v8.0.
#         A CPU-feature mismatch of the control's own making, unrelated to timers, and a
#         useful fact on its own: any named CPU model used instead of "-cpu max" must carry
#         PAuth (QNX's own runimage preset 9 is cortex-a710; 6.2 has no such model).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

